  "response": "Disputes of this kind are settled in practice by a layered procedure in which the trained eye, the archive and the instrument are brought to bear in turn. Connoisseurship comes first and carries real weight, since it rests on long comparative acquaintance with a master's handling of light, contour and impasto. Abraham Bredius authenticated \"The Supper at Emmaus\" as a work by Johannes Vermeer, and that verdict from the most authoritative connoisseur of the day led the Museum Boijmans Van Beuningen in Rotterdam to purchase the painting.\n\nThe documentary and the physical approaches answer different questions and must not be conflated. Provenance research traces the documented ownership history of a painting, whereas radiocarbon dating establishes the year of a painting's execution to within a single year. Neither procedure is self-sufficient, for a chain of ownership can be fabricated by an obliging dealer and a physical measurement can be misread by an incautious analyst.\n\nWhen the archive falls silent, the question passes to the laboratory bench, where the substance of the object is interrogated directly. Paul Coremans led the laboratory examination of the disputed Vermeers. It was his leadership of that examination that brought about the identification of phenol-formaldehyde resin in the paint layers of the disputed pictures. As a result of that identification, Van Meegeren stood trial in Amsterdam in 1947. The trial resulted in a sentence of one year in prison.\n\nThe probative force of such findings lies wholly in the chronology of artists' materials. Phenol-formaldehyde resin was first manufactured in the twentieth century; synthetic ultramarine, by contrast, became commercially available in the seventeenth century. The twentieth-century date at which the resin entered manufacture shows that the paint layers of the disputed picture contain a material unavailable to seventeenth-century painters. At least one of two marks of fabrication is accordingly present in the picture \u2014 a substance beyond the reach of any seventeenth-century studio in its paint film, or a craquelure artificially induced across its surface \u2014 and perhaps both are.\n\nThe presence of an anachronistic material in the paint film cannot be squared with the proposition that \"The Supper at Emmaus\" was executed during Vermeer's lifetime. Either the picture was executed during Vermeer's lifetime or it was executed after Vermeer's death; one of these must be the case and they cannot both be, and no refinement of stylistic sympathy can make the paint film older than the chemistry that produced it. That is why, when connoisseurial conviction and laboratory findings point in opposite directions, the material evidence is given the last word: a judgment of quality can be revised, but a pigment cannot be made to exist before it was invented.",
